====================================================================
OBSERVATIONS
====================================================================

1. **Seven patterns across three sizes.** On a 14-edge graph, the
   sweep identified:
   - size 2: 2-chain (4 instances), 2-star (4 instances)
   - size 3: 3-chain (2), 3-tree (1), 3-star (1), 3-cycle (1)
   - size 4: 4-chain (1 instance, the full 5-node chain as a
     4-edge structure)
   No cross-size duplicates because the registry persists across
   passes.

2. **Sizes do not interfere.** Each `autonomous_pass` call samples
   fresh (seed offset by size) and sees whatever meta-R exists at
   the time. Since discovery samples only data edges
   (meta-R excluded via `data_edges_sorted`), ADR 0010's meta-R
   additions from earlier sizes do not contaminate later-size
   sampling. Sizes run independently; only the registry carries
   state.

3. **Idempotence holds across the whole sweep.** Second sweep on
   `[2, 3, 4]` produces all `Existing`, zero `NewPattern`. RSet
   size unchanged. This is the composition of per-size
   `autonomous_pass` idempotence (ADR 0018).

4. **Meta-R growth is substantial.** From 14 to 83 edges (5.9×).
   With the default encoding (3 per-pattern + per-instance
   overhead), naming 16 total instances plus 7 pattern registry
   entries yields the observed delta. Acceptable for the scale;
   retraction (ADR 0020) is available if the bloat is unwanted.

5. **No MDL filter was applied.** Default policy allows singletons,
   which is why 3-tree, 3-cycle, 4-chain etc. with one instance
   each got named. A caller wanting "only reusable patterns" sets
   `naming.min_mdl_gain = 1` in the base config (ADR 0019).

====================================================================
WHAT THIS ENABLES
====================================================================

- Single-call "discover all pattern sizes in a range."
- Naturally composes with earlier ADRs: use MDL filter to only
  name reusable patterns at each size; use retraction if a sweep
  names something unwanted.
- Deterministic across runs: same seed + same sizes → same
  outcomes (verified via the per-size seed offset).

